  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>>>|<label|fig:mass-graphs>The
  mass equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:mass> and the two divergent parts of the bare
  mass. The cross is the insertion <math|R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2>>. Since
  <math|D<rsup|4>h<rsup|[2]>(0)> contains the bubble of
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:seed-rings>, its contraction contains the
  sunset. Integrating in <math|q> turns the crossed line into a line
  <math|C>: the tadpole is linear in <math|N> and the sunset logarithmic.>

  Power counting (Exercise<nbsp><hlink|A.3|cqft_lecture4.tm#ex:power-counting>
  of Lecture<nbsp>4) says that no other counterterm is needed. In the flow
  this is the statement that the integrals<nbsp><eqref|eq:iteration> of all
  vertices with <math|k\<geqslant\>4> legs converge at <math|q=\<infty\>>:
  only the quadratic jet must be fixed by a boundary condition.

  There is a subtlety: the running mass <math|\<mu\><rsub|q>> at fixed
  <math|q> has a limit as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, but
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>=\<mu\><rsub|\<infty\>>> does not. One never
  interchanges these two limits. Also, the <em|whole> running kernel
  <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>> is kept: replacing the sunset by its local part
  would leave a nonlocal remainder of the wrong size.

  <subsection|Two correction steps>

  Why two corrections, and not one or three? The answer is in the backward
  equation of<nbsp><eqref|eq:YZ-fbsde>. Freeze the noise and the large
  fields, and estimate the size of <math|Z> produced by a defect on the ball.
  The propagator of <math|\<mathd\>Z=-H*Z<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+\<ldots\>>
  from <math|q> to <math|s> is about <math|s/q> (since
  <math|H\<approx\>C<rsub|q>> has norm <math|q<rsup|-1>>), so

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Z-heuristic>Z<rsub|q>\<approx\><big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q>*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s,<space|2em><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<lesssim\><around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|s><rsup|3>\<lesssim\>s<rsup|-1><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|s><rsup|3>,
  </equation>

  where we used that the low jets of <math|e> vanish, so on the ball
  <math|<around|\||D*e|\|>\<leqslant\><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e|)><around|\||y|\|><rsup|3>/6>.
  After <math|n> correction steps the defect is the trace difference of the
  last two approximations, of relative size <math|g<rsup|n+1>>: by the trace
  estimate,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|s><rsup|n+1>*s<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|s><rsup|-4>=\<gamma\><rsup|n+3>*s<rsup|-<around|(|n+4|)>/2>,<space|2em><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<lesssim\>\<gamma\><rsup|n+3/2>*s<rsup|-<around|(|n+3|)>/2>,
  </equation*>

  and therefore

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||Z<rsub|q>|\|>\<lesssim\><frac|\<gamma\><rsup|n+3/2>|q>*<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>s<rsup|-<around|(|n+1|)>/2>\<mathd\>s.
  </equation*>

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|steps
    <math|n>>|<cell|<math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsub|s>|)>>>|<cell|<math|<around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>>>|<cell|<math|<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<mathd\>s>>>|<row|<cell|<math|0>
    (seed only)>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3/2>*s<rsup|-3/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (power)>>|<row|<cell|<math|1>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|4>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5/2>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (logarithm)>>|<row|<cell|<math|2>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5>*s<rsup|-3>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|2*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-3/2>>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  With one step the bounded-field source produces a logarithmically divergent
  error; two steps are the minimum, and they suffice:
  <math|Z<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>=O<around|(|q<rsup|-3/2>|)>>, and the
  backward energy <math|\<gamma\><rsup|-1>*<big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\||Z<rsup|<math-up|b>>|\|><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q>
  is finite (Lecture<nbsp>6). The general (fractional) theory gives the depth
  criterion <math|<around|(|n+2|)>*\<varkappa\>\<gtr\><around|(|3+\<varkappa\>|)>/2>,
  with subcriticality <math|\<varkappa\>=<frac|1|2>> for
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: <math|<around|(|2+2|)>\<cdot\><frac|1|2>=2\<gtr\><frac|7|4>>,
  while <math|n=1> gives <math|<frac|3|2>\<less\><frac|7|4>>. This is a
  sufficient condition, which loses <math|\<varkappa\>/2>; for
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the heuristic above shows that
  <math|n\<gtr\>1> suffices, with <math|n=1> borderline.

  The two senses of \Ptwo renormalisations\Q are related. The mass
  counterterm must contain the sunset because the quartic vertex of the
  corrections, already of the seed, contains the bubble, and contracting it
  produces the sunset; and the second step is needed because the defect of
  the first one, the three-loop remainder, is borderline in the backward
  equation, exactly as the sunset is borderline in the mass.

  <subsection|The explicit counterterm><label|sec:counter>

  Formula (<reference|eq:bare-mass>) gives <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> as an output
  of the flow, from the boundary value <math|b>. The input can be made
  explicit, and this also fixes what the finite part is.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|The explicit counterterm><label|thm:counter>Let
    <math|a<rsub|2>=<math-up|Tr> C<rsub|q><rsup|2>> and
    <math|C<rsub|q><rsup|(2)>> be the kernel of <math|C<rsub|q><rsup|2>>. For
    <math|\|b\|\<leqslant\> B\<gamma\> q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|1/2>> and
    <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> large,

    <\equation>
      \<beta\><rsub|q>=-\<gamma\> a<rsub|2>-\<gamma\>\<mu\><rsub|q>a<rsub|2><rprime|'>+\<gamma\><rsup|2>a<rsub|2><rsup|2>+2\<gamma\><rsup|2><big|int>
      C<rsub|q><rsup|2>C<rsub|q><rsup|(2)>+\<cal-R\><rsub|q>,<space|1em>\|\<cal-R\><rsub|q>\|\<leqslant\>
      C\<gamma\><rsup|3>q<rsup|-3/2>,<label|eq:beta-exp>
    </equation>

    and so, for <math|f<rsub|N>(b)=\<mu\><rsub|N>(b)+3\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>
    C-6\<lambda\><rsup|2><big|int> C<rsup|3>>,

    <\equation>
      f<rsub|N>(b)=b(1+\<gamma\> a<rsub|2>(q<rsub|\<ast\>>))+\<gamma\>(<math-up|Tr>
      C-<math-up|Tr> C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>)-6\<lambda\><rsup|2><big|int>(C<rsup|3>-C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|3>)+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|\<infty\>>(\<cal-R\><rsub|q>+\<gamma\>(\<beta\><rsub|q>+\<gamma\>
      a<rsub|2>)a<rsub|2>)<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> q.<label|eq:fN>
    </equation>

    Moreover <math|\|f<rsub|N>(b<rsub|1>)-f<rsub|N>(b<rsub|2>)-(b<rsub|1>-b<rsub|2>)\|\<leqslant\>
    C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>\|b<rsub|1>-b<rsub|2>\|>, and
    <math|f<rsub|N>\<to\> f> pointwise along the whole sequence.
    Consequently, for <math|B> above a universal constant, every
    <math|r\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> large depending on
    <math|\<lambda\>,q<rsub|0>,B,r>, there is a unique <math|b<rsub|N>> with

    <\equation>
      \<mu\><rsub|N>=r-3\<lambda\><math-up|Tr> C+6\<lambda\><rsup|2><big|int>
      C<rsup|3>,<label|eq:explicit>
    </equation>

    and <math|b<rsub|N>> converges along the whole sequence.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    <strong|Expansion.> Expand <math|R<rsub|0>=C-C*M*C+C*M*C*M*R<rsub|0>>
    with <math|C=C<rsub|q>>. Since <math|a<rsub|2><rprime|'>=-2<math-up|Tr>
    C<rsup|3>>, the first term of (<reference|eq:mass>) is <math|-\<gamma\>
    a<rsub|2>-\<gamma\>\<mu\> a<rsub|2><rprime|'>+2\<gamma\><math-up|Tr>(C<rsup|3>\<Theta\>)>,
    up to <math|O(\<gamma\> a<rsub|2>g<rsub|q><rsup|2>)>, and
    <math|\|<math-up|Tr>(C<rsup|3>\<Theta\>)\|\<leqslant\>
    C*q<rsup|-1>v<rsub|1>(\<Theta\>)> because
    <math|\|<wide|\<Theta\>|^>(k)\|\<leqslant\>2\|k\|v<rsub|1>>. The
    corrections in <math|h<rsup|[2]>> give
    <math|O(\<gamma\><rsup|3>q<rsup|-3/2>)> after contraction, by
    (<reference|eq:increments>). The quartic part of the seed is
    <math|-<frac|\<gamma\><rsup|2>|4><big|int><big|int>
    C<rsup|2>(x,z)y(x)<rsup|2>y(z)<rsup|2>>, and contracting its Hessian with
    <math|R<rsub|0><rsup|2>> gives the operator
    <math|\<gamma\><rsup|2>a<rsub|2><math-up|diag>(R<rsub|0><rsup|2>)+2\<gamma\><rsup|2>C<rsup|2>\<cdot\>
    R<rsub|0><rsup|2>>, where <math|\<cdot\>> is the pointwise product of
    kernels. Its constant-mode eigenvalue, with <math|R<rsub|0><rsup|2>>
    replaced by <math|C<rsup|2>> at a relative cost <math|g<rsub|q>>, gives
    the remaining two terms.

    <strong|Integration.> Use <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><math-up|Tr>
    C<rsub|q>=-a<rsub|2>>, <math|\<partial\><rsub|q><big|int>
    C<rsub|q><rsup|3>=-3<big|int> C<rsub|q><rsup|2>C<rsub|q><rsup|(2)>>, and
    <math|-\<gamma\>\<mu\> a<rsub|2><rprime|'>+\<gamma\><rsup|2>a<rsub|2><rsup|2>=-\<gamma\>\<partial\><rsub|q>(\<mu\>
    a<rsub|2>)+\<gamma\>(\<beta\>+\<gamma\> a<rsub|2>)a<rsub|2>>. This is the
    cancellation of the double-tadpole logarithms: <math|\<beta\>+\<gamma\>
    a<rsub|2>=O(\<gamma\><rsup|2>/q)>. At fixed <math|N> the boundary terms
    at <math|q=\<infty\>> vanish, which gives (<reference|eq:fN>).

    <strong|Response.> For two boundary values with difference
    <math|\<delta\> b>, put <math|D=sup<rsub|q>\|\<delta\>\<mu\><rsub|q>\|>
    and <math|X=sup<rsub|q>q<rsup|3/2>v<rsub|1>(\<delta\>\<Theta\><rsub|q>)>,
    which are finite at fixed <math|N>. The trace comparison gives
    <math|\|\<delta\>\<beta\><rsub|q>\|\<leqslant\>
    C\<gamma\>(D*q<rsup|-3/2>+X*q<rsup|-5/2>)> and <math|v<rsub|1>(\<delta\>
    B<rsub|q>-\<delta\>\<beta\><rsub|q>I)\<leqslant\>
    C\<gamma\><rsup|2>(D*q<rsup|-5/2>+X*q<rsup|-7/2>)>, so
    <math|X\<leqslant\> C\<gamma\><rsup|2>D+C\<gamma\><rsup|2>q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1>X>
    and <math|D\<leqslant\>\|\<delta\> b\|+C\<gamma\>
    q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-1/2>D+C\<gamma\> q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|-3/2>X>.
    Hence <math|D\<leqslant\>2\|\<delta\> b\|>, and integrating
    <math|\<delta\>\<beta\>> gives the claim.

    <strong|Limit and inversion.> In (<reference|eq:fN>), <math|<math-up|Tr>
    C-<math-up|Tr> C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>> has summable terms
    <math|\<lesssim\>(1+\|k\|)<rsup|-4>>; <math|C-C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    converges uniformly and <math|C<rsup|2>+C*C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>+C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|2>>
    in <math|L<rsup|1>>; and the integrand converges at fixed <math|q> by
    consistency of the coefficients, dominated by
    <math|C\<gamma\><rsup|3>q<rsup|-3/2>>. As <math|<math-up|Tr>
    C-<math-up|Tr> C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>
    C+q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|1/2>/10> uniformly in <math|N>, the range of
    <math|f<rsub|N>> contains <math|r> for <math|B> above a universal
    constant and <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> large. Monotonicity and pointwise
    convergence give the convergence of <math|b<rsub|N>>.
  </proof>

  The measure depends on <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> only, so
  (<reference|eq:explicit>) does not depend on the choice of
  <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The boundary value <math|b> is not the finite part:
  by (<reference|eq:fN>), <math|f<rsub|N>(b)\<approx\> b+\<gamma\>
  q<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|1/2>/(4\<pi\>)> at leading order, so at fixed
  <math|b>, raising <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> changes the theory.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Counting loops><label|ex:loop-count> (a) Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:loop>
    to show that a loop with <math|p> vertices
    <math|D<rsup|j<rsub|i>+2>*<around|(|U+h|)>>,
    <math|j<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1>, each with
    <math|s\<leqslant\>C*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-j<rsub|i>>>, and
    <math|k=<big|sum>j<rsub|i>> external legs has rooted norm
    <math|\<leqslant\>C*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>q*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>\<leqslant\>C*q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>>.
    (b) Show that <math|q\<cdot\>q<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|q><rsup|-k>=g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>>.
    (c) If one vertex is replaced by a vertex of size
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*<space|0.17em>q*r<rsub|q><rsup|-j>>, show that the
    loop gains the factor <math|\<varepsilon\>>. Deduce the sizes
    <math|g<rsup|j+1>> of the increments in<nbsp><eqref|eq:increments>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The bubble>Compute <math|D<rsup|4>*\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|0|)>>.
    Show that its rooted norm is <math|6*\<gamma\><rsup|2>*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>>
    and its first spatial moment is <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsup|2>*q<rsup|-1>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Small Hessians are not enough>On one site take
    <math|q=\<gamma\>=1>, <math|M=0>, fixed small
    <math|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|n><around|(|y|)>=\<alpha\>*n<rsup|-2>*<around|(|cos
    <around|(|n*y|)>-1+n<rsup|2>*y<rsup|2>/2|)>>,
    <math|h<rsub|n>=<wide|h|^><rsub|n>+<frac|\<delta\>|12>*y<rsup|4>>. Show
    that both have small Hessians on <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<less\>2>, that
    <math|h<rsub|n>-<wide|h|^><rsub|n>> has bounded derivatives, but
    <math|D<rsup|4>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><around|[|h<rsub|n>|]>-\<cal-T\><around|[|<wide|h|^><rsub|n>|]>|)><around|(|0|)>=12*<around|(|2*\<delta\>+\<alpha\>*\<delta\>*n<rsup|2>+\<delta\><rsup|2>|)>>
    is unbounded in <math|n>. Conclude that the trace estimate needs the
    individual higher derivative bounds.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Why two steps>Verify the table above. With one step, show that the
    bounded-field part of <math|Z> is of size <math|q<rsup|-1>*log
    <around|(|T/q|)>> on <math|<around|[|q,T|]>>, for an upper scale cutoff
    <math|T>.
  </exercise>

  <section|The flow at large fields><label|sec:large-fields>

  <subsection|The large-field problem>

  All the estimates of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:coeff> hold on the
  field ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>2*r<rsub|q>>,
  and only there. But the forecast <math|Y<rsub|q>> is a random field on
  <math|N<rsup|3>> sites, and nothing prevents it from being larger than
  <math|r<rsub|q>> somewhere. The free field has typical size
  <math|q<rsup|1/4>\<ll\>r<rsub|q>>, but it exceeds <math|r<rsub|q>> with
  small, nonzero probability, and nothing a priori controls the interacting
  forecast. We need an action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> defined
  and <em|uniformly convex for all fields>, which agrees with
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>> on the ball.

  <paragraph|A scalar model> The difficulty is already visible in the
  one-site seed, <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|[|log
  <around|(|1+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|)>-\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|]>>. Its power
  series <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>2><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>|2*j>*<around|(|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>/q|)><rsup|j>>
  has radius of convergence exactly <math|r<rsub|q>>: the singularities are
  at <math|y=\<pm\>\<mathi\>r<rsub|q>>. Truncating it at any order produces a
  polynomial whose top coefficient has sign <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j+1>>:
  for even <math|j\<geqslant\>4> the truncation is unbounded below and
  destroys the convexity of <math|U+\<cal-L\>> at
  <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<gg\>r<rsub|q>>. The exact function is harmless: its
  logarithmic part grows only logarithmically, and
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|q><rprime|''><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|9|8>*\<gamma\>/q\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>/q>
  for all <math|y>. In field theory we do not have the exact function, only
  bounds on the ball, obtained through an expansion of the trace in vertices
  (the corrections are not polynomials, but their estimates are expansions in
  the field). This is the <em|large-field problem>: perturbative control
  holds for <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<lesssim\>r<rsub|q>>, and outside one
  needs a non-perturbative replacement that preserves the structure the
  estimates use.

  The structure to preserve is coercivity, and it is carried by the bare
  quartic term. In the scalar model the coordinate equation
  <math|q*y+\<lambda\>*y<rsup|3>=p> has noise coefficient

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|q>\<wedge\><frac|1|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>>:
  </equation*>

  large fields are <em|more> elliptic and less noisy. A good large-field
  extension keeps <math|U> untouched and freezes only the correction.

  <subsection|Clipping>

  Let <math|\<tau\>:\<bbb-R\>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>> be smooth and odd,
  <math|\<tau\><around|(|u|)>=u> on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>, constant
  outside <math|<around|[|-2,2|]>>, with <math|0\<leqslant\>\<tau\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>1>,
  and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:clipping>\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)><around|(|x|)>\<assign\>r<rsub|q>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\>*<around*|(|y<around|(|x|)>/r<rsub|q>|)>,<space|2em><wide|h|~><rsub|q>\<assign\>h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>,<space|2em><wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<assign\>U+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+<wide|h|~><rsub|q>.
  </equation>

  The clip acts pointwise in space and maps every field into the ball. The
  quartic and quadratic parts are not clipped. By the chain rule, with
  factors <math|<around|\||\<kappa\><rsub|q><rsup|<around|(|b|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsub|q><rsup|1-b>>,
  the clipped correction obeys the same bounds<nbsp><eqref|eq:power-counting>
  <em|globally>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:global-h>s*<around|(|D<rsup|j>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|q>*q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-j>*<around|(|j=2,3,4|)>,<space|2em>s*<around|(|D<rsup|2>*\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|q>,
  </equation>

  for all <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>>; the scale derivative includes the motion
  of the clip, <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\><rsub|q>=O<around|(|r<rsub|q>/q|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Global coercivity><label|lem:resolvent> For <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>
    large, for all <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>> and
    <math|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>>,

    <eqnarray*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|c*<space|0.17em><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>C*<space|0.17em><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)>,>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<leqslant\><frac|1+C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>|q>*<space|1em><around|(|1\<leqslant\>p\<leqslant\>\<infty\>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|diag>H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>.>|<eq-number><label|eq:resolvent>>>>>
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Idea>
    <math|H<rsup|-1>=K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>+M<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>>,
    and <math|M<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>> is small relative to
    <math|K<rsub|q>>: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|q>*q>,
    and <math|M<rsub|q>> is small after multiplication by <math|C<rsub|q>>
    (the centred kernel is controlled by its first moment,
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:receive>). Write
    <math|H=<around|(|I+G*<around|(|M+D<rsup|2>*<wide|h|~>|)>|)><rsup|-1>*G>
    with <math|G=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> and use
    the Neumann series and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:free>.
  </render-proof>

  So <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> is a globally convex action, and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:YZ> applies.

  <subsection|The complete defect>

  On the set <math|<around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|q>|}>>
  the clip is the identity and the defect is <math|e<rsup|<math-up|o>>>, of
  size<nbsp><eqref|eq:defect-bound>. Outside it, the clipped action does not
  solve the Wetterich equation even approximately in relative terms. For
  <math|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>> split the drift into its bounded-field
  and large-field parts,

  <\equation*>
    b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>-H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>D*\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>><rsub|q>*<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|q>*y|)>,<space|2em>b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>\<assign\>b<rsub|q>-b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>,
  </equation*>

  and let <math|\<chi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>>>
  (pointwise) and <math|t<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=y*<space|0.17em>\<chi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>,
  the field restricted to the large-field set.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Complete source bounds<nbsp><cite|FBSDE26>><label|prop:source> For
    <math|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, uniformly in
    <math|y\<in\>H<rsub|N>>, <math|N> and
    <math|p\<in\><around|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,

    <align|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|p>+r<rsub|q><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>*y<rsup|2>*\<chi\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>.<eq-number><label|eq:source>>>>>>

    Moreover <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.
  </proposition>

  The first bound is<nbsp><eqref|eq:Z-heuristic> with <math|n=2>: on the ball
  <math|<around|\||D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|e<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)>*<around|(|2*r|)><rsup|3>/6>,
  and <math|q<rsup|-1>*\<gamma\><rsup|5>*q<rsup|-3>*r<rsub|q><rsup|3>=\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>>.
  The large-field part is as large as the bound
  <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around|\||y|\|>=<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>>
  on the whole drift (no smallness beyond the factor <math|g<rsub|q>> already
  present in <math|b>), but it is <em|supported on the large-field set> (up
  to nonlocal tails controlled by operator bounds). The last bound follows
  from the second since on <math|<around|{|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r|}>>,
  <math|<around|\||t|\|>+r<rsup|-1>*y<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*r<rsup|-2><around|\||t|\|><rsup|3>>,
  and <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>*r<rsup|-2>|)><rsup|2>=\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5>>.

  <paragraph|Where the large-field error comes from> Compute the gradient of
  the defect of the clipped action and compare it with
  <math|D*\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>D*e<rsup|<math-up|o>>*<around|(|\<kappa\>*y|)>>.
  With <math|z=\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>,
  <math|J=D*\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>> (multiplication by
  <math|\<tau\><rprime|'>*<around|(|y/r|)>>),
  <math|h=h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>>,
  <math|M<rprime|'>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*M<rsub|q>>, and the unclipped
  response <math|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>=<around|(|K+M+\<gamma\>*z<rsup|2>+D<rsup|2>*h<around|(|z|)>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  the difference has four groups (seven terms):

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item>the mass term <math|M<rprime|'>*y-J*M<rprime|'>*z>: the quadratic
    part is not clipped, but its flow was computed at <math|z>;

    <item>the motion of the clip, <math|J*D<rsup|2>*h<around|(|z|)>*\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\>+<around|(|D*\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<kappa\>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*D*h<around|(|z|)>>;

    <item><math|\<gamma\>*<around|(|y-J*z|)><math-up|diag>H<rsup|2>> and
    <math|\<gamma\>*J*z<math-up|diag><around|(|H<rsup|2>-<around|(|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)><rsup|2>|)>>:
    the quartic vertex sees <math|y>, the correction sees <math|z>, and the
    two responses differ by <math|\<gamma\>*<around|(|y<rsup|2>-z<rsup|2>|)>>
    and by the chain-rule terms of the clipped Hessian, all supported on
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>|}>>;

    <item>the contraction of <math|D<rsup|3>*<around|(|h\<circ\>\<kappa\>|)>-J*D<rsup|3>*h<around|(|z|)>>
    (the chain rule produces terms with <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''>>,
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'''>>, supported on large fields) and of
    <math|D<rsup|3>*h> with <math|H<rsup|2>-<around|(|H<rsup|<math-up|o>>|)><rsup|2>>.
  </enumerate-roman>

  Each group vanishes if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|q>>,
  and each is bounded by <math|g<rsub|q>> times <math|<around|\||t|\|>> or
  <math|r<rsup|-1>*y<rsup|2>*\<chi\>>, in <math|L<rsup|p>>, by the same loop
  estimates (<cite|FBSDE26>; Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:clip-scalar> for the
  one-site version). There is no cancellation to exploit: the clipping error
  is genuinely of the size <math|<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>> of
  the whole drift on the large-field set. What saves us is that this set is
  rarely visited, and its occupation is controlled by the coercivity of the
  actual dynamics. Lecture<nbsp>6 proves exactly this, with the weighted
  <em|large-field action>

  <\equation*>
    <big|int><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|\<infty\>>q<rsup|-3/2>*<space|0.17em>\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q,
  </equation*>

  (the <math|t>-part of the large-field action of Lecture<nbsp>6), whose
  weight is matched to the last bound in<nbsp><eqref|eq:source>:
  <math|q<rsup|7/2>\<cdot\>\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5>=\<gamma\><rsup|3>\<cdot\><around|(|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>|)>>,
  so <math|<big|int>q<rsup|7/2><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>\<mathd\>q>,
  where <math|q<rsup|7/2>> is the weight with which
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>
  enters the backward representation (Lecture<nbsp>6).

  <paragraph|The coordinate correction> Finally, write the approximate
  coordinate equation as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coordinate>K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*<around*|(|Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<tfrac|\<gamma\>|3>Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>,<space|2em>\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<assign\>C<rsub|q>*<around*|(|M<rsub|q>*y+D*<wide|h|~><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)>,
  </equation>

  using <math|D*U<around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*y<rsup|3>=<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*y<rsup|3>>.
  The correction is a small contraction, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>\<assign\>C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>>,
  for all <math|y> and <math|1\<leqslant\>p\<leqslant\>\<infty\>>, and
  <math|\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|0|)>=0> by evenness. So, up to a small
  Lipschitz perturbation, <em|the forecast is the solution of the cubic
  elliptic equation <math|K<rsub|q>*<around|(|Y+\<varrho\>|)>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*Y<rsup|3>=K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>>
  driven by the field at resolution <math|q>>. This is the equation whose
  coercivity Lecture<nbsp>6 exploits.

  <subsection|Why the Polchinski expansion is not
  enough><label|sec:polchinski-large>

  Lecture<nbsp>4 gives two exact descriptions of the flow: the Polchinski
  equation for the force <math|F> at the field <math|\<Phi\>>, and the
  Wetterich equation at the forecast <math|\<psi\>>. We use the second, here
  and in Lecture<nbsp>6. The first looks more economical, and the following
  programme is natural. Keep the bare cube
  <math|-\<lambda\>\<varphi\><rsup|3>> unclipped, so that the approximate
  force stays coercive at all fields. Expand the rest of the force in
  perturbation theory and clip only these higher orders. Pay for the clipping
  in the source of the equation for the remainder <math|R=F-f>, using the a
  priori estimates. No Legendre transform would be needed.

  Here we test the programme where everything can be computed: on one site.
  There is no spatial structure and no renormalisation, so whatever goes
  wrong has nothing to do with divergences. The result is that a frozen cube
  is the wrong approximation at large fields: the remainder must contain a
  cube of its own, and it cancels the coercivity that the programme relies
  on. The cure is to integrate the cube, that is, to solve the transport part
  of the flow exactly. This produces a global approximation with a uniformly
  small error, and it is precisely the forecast coordinate of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:YZ>.

  <strong|Conventions.> As in Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:one-site>:
  <math|N=1>, <math|\<Delta\>=0>, <math|K<rsub|q>=q>,
  <math|V(\<varphi\>)=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>\<varphi\><rsup|4>>,
  <math|\<gamma\>=3\<lambda\>> and <math|r<rsub|q>=(q/\<gamma\>)<rsup|1/2>>.
  We write <math|S=C<rsub|q>=1/q> for the remaining covariance, and <math|x>
  for the value of the field at resolution <math|q>.

  <paragraph|The exact force is linear at large fields> The effective
  potential and the force are

  <\equation*>
    V<rsub|q>(x)=-log \<bbb-E\>exp<around|(|-<frac|\<lambda\>|4>(x+S<rsup|1/2>\<xi\>)<rsup|4>|)>,<space|1em>
    F<rsub|q>=-V<rsub|q><rprime|'>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|\<xi\>> a standard Gaussian, and they solve the Polchinski and
  Burgers equations

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|q>V+<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>V<rprime|''>=<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>(V<rprime|'>)<rsup|2>,<space|1em>
    \<partial\><rsub|q>F+<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>F<rprime|''>+S<rsup|2>F*F<rprime|'>=0.<label|eq:pl-burgers>
  </equation>

  Let <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>> be the expectation for the probability density
  proportional to <math|exp(-<frac|\<lambda\>|4>y<rsup|4>-<frac|q|2>(y-x)<rsup|2>)>,
  and <math|m<rsub|q>(x)=\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y>. Differentiating
  <math|V<rsub|q>>, and integrating by parts in <math|y>, gives

  <\equation>
    F<rsub|q>(x)=-\<lambda\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|3>=q(m<rsub|q>(x)-x).<label|eq:pl-tweedie>
  </equation>

  This is (<reference|eq:polchinski-fbsde>): the posterior mean is the
  forecast, <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=m<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)>. So
  <math|F<rsub|q>> measures how far the forecast lies behind the field.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Posterior mean><label|lem:pl-posterior>For all <math|x> and
    <math|q>, <math|\|m<rsub|q>(x)\|\<leqslant\>
    2\<lambda\><rsup|-1/4>+2(q\|x\|/\<lambda\>)<rsup|1/3>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Integrating the derivative of <math|y> times the density gives
    <math|\<lambda\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|4>+q\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|2>-q*x\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y=1>.
    With <math|M=(\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|4>)<rsup|1/4>> this gives
    <math|\<lambda\> M<rsup|4>\<leqslant\> 1+q\|x\|M>, and Young's inequality
    gives <math|<frac|\<lambda\>|2>M<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>
    1+(q\|x\|)<rsup|4/3>(2\<lambda\>)<rsup|-1/3>>. Finally
    <math|\|m<rsub|q>\|\<leqslant\> M>.
  </proof>

  Therefore, by (<reference|eq:pl-tweedie>), the two regimes of the force are

  <\equation>
    F<rsub|q>(x)\<to\>-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3><space|1em>(<text|fixed >x,
    q\<to\>\<infty\>),<space|1em> F<rsub|q>(x)=-q*x+O(\|x\|<rsup|1/3>)<space|1em>(<text|fixed
    >q, \|x\|\<to\>\<infty\>).<label|eq:pl-two-limits>
  </equation>

  The limits cannot be interchanged. The crossover is at <math|\|x\|\<sim\>
  r<rsub|q>>, where the cube equals the Gaussian restoring force:
  <math|\<lambda\> r<rsub|q><rsup|3>\<sim\> q*r<rsub|q>>. Beyond it the force
  is linear, because convolution with a Gaussian of variance <math|S> cannot
  produce a force steeper than <math|x/S>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The remainder of a frozen cube><label|prop:pl-frozen>Let
    <math|f(x)=-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>+f<rsub|1>(x)> with
    <math|f<rsub|1>(x)=o(\|x\|<rsup|3>)>, for instance linear terms plus
    bounded clipped corrections. At every fixed <math|q>, the remainder
    <math|R<rsub|q>=F<rsub|q>-f> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <frac|R<rsub|q>(x)|\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>>\<to\>1<space|1em>(\|x\|\<to\>\<infty\>).
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  So no estimate can make <math|R> a small fraction of the retained cube
  uniformly in the field. The cube in <math|R> is not a new counterterm,
  since there is nothing to renormalise here. It is the compensation for
  having frozen the leading term of a force that does not stay cubic.

  <paragraph|The field equation does not see the cube> The field equation
  <hlink|(18.18)|cqft_lecture6.tm#eq:field-q> of Lecture<nbsp>6 on one site
  is <math|q<rsub|0>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=J<rsub|q>F<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)+\<eta\><rsub|q>>,
  where <math|J<rsub|q>=(q-q<rsub|0>)/q>. Its noise is the filtered
  measurement noise, so <math|\<bbb-E\>exp(h\<eta\><rsub|q>)\<leqslant\>exp((q-q<rsub|0>)h<rsup|2>/2)>
  whatever the interaction (Lemma<nbsp><hlink|13.2|cqft_lecture6.tm#lem:noise>
  of Lecture<nbsp>6). With the frozen cube <math|f=-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>> the
  equation looks coercive:

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|0>x+J<rsub|q>\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>=J<rsub|q>R<rsub|q>(x)+\<eta\><rsub|q>(x).
  </equation*>

  But by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:pl-frozen>,
  <math|J<rsub|q>R<rsub|q>> cancels the cube on the left side at large
  fields. Inserting (<reference|eq:pl-tweedie>) shows what is left:

  <\equation>
    q*x=(q-q<rsub|0>)m<rsub|q>(x)+\<eta\><rsub|q>(x).<label|eq:pl-linear>
  </equation>

  There is still confinement, but it is linear, and it comes from the
  sublinear growth of <math|m<rsub|q>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Tails on one site><label|prop:pl-tails>If
    <math|q<rsup|2>\<geqslant\>24\<lambda\>>, then
    <math|\|m<rsub|q>(x)\|\<leqslant\><frac|9|10>\|x\|> for
    <math|\|x\|\<geqslant\> r<rsub|q>>, and for <math|R\<geqslant\>
    r<rsub|q>>

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-P\>(\|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\|\<geqslant\>
      R)\<leqslant\>2exp<around|(|-<frac|q<rsup|2>R<rsup|2>|200(q-q<rsub|0>)>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The mean <math|m> has the sign of <math|x>, the density being an even
    density tilted by <math|\<mathe\><rsup|q*x*y>>. Jensen's inequality in
    the identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:pl-posterior> gives
    <math|\<lambda\> m<rsup|4>+q*m<rsup|2>-q*x*m\<leqslant\>1>. If
    <math|x\<geqslant\> r<rsub|q>> and <math|m\<gtr\><frac|9|10>x>, then
    <math|\<lambda\> m<rsup|3>+q(m-x)\<gtr\>*x(<frac|729|1000>\<lambda\>
    x<rsup|2>-<frac|q|10>)\<geqslant\><frac|143|1000>q*x>, as
    <math|\<lambda\> x<rsup|2>\<geqslant\> q/3>, so the left side is at least
    <math|<frac|1287|10000>q*x<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><frac|429|10000>q<rsup|2>/\<lambda\>\<gtr\>1>,
    a contradiction. So <math|\|\<eta\><rsub|q>(x)\|\<geqslant\><frac|q|10>\|x\|>
    by (<reference|eq:pl-linear>), and we conclude with the sub-Gaussian
    bound.
  </proof>

  At <math|R=r<rsub|q>> the exponent is at least
  <math|q<rsup|2>/(600\<lambda\>)>. This estimate treats the combined
  restoring force through the posterior moment. It never uses the cube as a
  coercive term against a remainder regarded as a source.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|This does not transfer to the lattice>With a quadratic term
    <math|<frac|r|2>y<rsup|2>> in the action,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:pl-posterior> acquires the term
    <math|((q+r)<rsub|->/\<lambda\>)<rsup|1/2>>. On the lattice the bare mass
    makes <math|r> divergent and negative, so this elementary moment bound is
    not uniform until the renormalised cancellations have been built in. The
    scalar tail bound is therefore not an independent input for the field
    theory.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|What clipping does to the Polchinski equation> For a potential
  <math|U> put <math|D<rsub|q>=\<partial\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>\<partial\><rsub|x><rsup|2>>,
  <math|B(U,U)=<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>(U<rprime|'>)<rsup|2>> and
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U)=D<rsub|q>U-B(U,U)>. So
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(V<rsub|q>)=0>, and the force <math|f=-U<rprime|'>>
  has Burgers defect <math|E=\<partial\><rsub|q>f+<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>f<rprime|''>+S<rsup|2>f*f<rprime|'>=-\<partial\><rsub|x>\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U)>.
  Perturbation theory solves

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|q>U<rsub|1>=0,<space|1em> D<rsub|q>U<rsub|2>=B(U<rsub|1>,U<rsub|1>),
  </equation*>

  with <math|U<rsub|1>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>(x<rsup|4>+6S*x<rsup|2>+3S<rsup|2>)>,
  whose leading term is the cube in the force, and
  <math|U<rsub|2>=-<frac|1|2>\<lambda\><rsup|2>S*x<rsup|6>+\<ldots\>>, the
  first tree. Keep <math|U<rsub|1>> unclipped and clip the correction:
  <math|U<rsup|c>=U<rsub|1>+U<rsub|2>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>>, with the
  clip <math|\<kappa\><rsub|q>> of (<reference|eq:clipping>). Writing
  <math|\<cal-C\>*U<rsub|2>=U<rsub|2>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>>, a direct
  expansion gives the exact identity

  <\equation>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U<rsup|c>)=(\<cal-C\>-1)B(U<rsub|1>,U<rsub|1>)+[D<rsub|q>,\<cal-C\>]U<rsub|2>-2B(U<rsub|1>,\<cal-C\>*U<rsub|2>)-B(\<cal-C\>*U<rsub|2>,\<cal-C\>*U<rsub|2>).<label|eq:pl-clip-defect>
  </equation>

  Without the clip only the last two terms, of higher order, would be
  present. The clip reintroduces a defect at exactly the order that
  <math|U<rsub|2>> was built to cancel. For
  <math|\|x\|\<geqslant\>2r<rsub|q>> the term <math|\<cal-C\>*U<rsub|2>> does
  not depend on <math|x>, so <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U<rsup|c>)=c(q)-<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>(U<rsub|1><rprime|'>)<rsup|2>>,
  and the force defect is exactly

  <\equation>
    E<rsub|q>(x)=S<rsup|2>U<rsub|1><rprime|'>(x)U<rsub|1><rprime|''>(x)=3\<lambda\><rsup|2>S<rsup|2>x<rsup|5>(1+O(S/x<rsup|2>)).<label|eq:pl-quintic>
  </equation>

  This is the price of the clipping. At the edge of the clip it has size
  <math|(q/\<lambda\>)<rsup|1/2>>, which is not integrable in <math|q>, so it
  can only be paid through the small probability of reaching the clip. Along
  the exact process <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
  \<Phi\><rsub|q>=S<rsup|2>F<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
  q+S<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W<rsub|q>>, Itô's formula gives for
  <math|R<rsub|q>=F<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)-f<rsub|q><rsup|c>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)>
  the equation

  <\equation>
    <space|0.17em>\<mathd\> R<rsub|q>=-[E<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)+S<rsup|2>(f<rsub|q><rsup|c>)<rprime|'>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)R<rsub|q>]<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W<rsub|q>.<label|eq:pl-remainder>
  </equation>

  The transport term has a favourable sign at large fields:
  <math|(f<rsup|c>)<rprime|'>=-3\<lambda\>(x<rsup|2>+S)> for
  <math|\|x\|\<geqslant\>2r<rsub|q>>, which is the dissipation one hopes to
  use (near the clip the sign fails: on the ball the quintic takes over
  beyond <math|\|x\|\<approx\>0.77r<rsub|q>>). But at large fields the
  bracket is <math|3\<lambda\><rsup|2>S<rsup|2>x<rsup|5>-3\<lambda\>
  S<rsup|2>x<rsup|2>R>, and for the true <math|R\<sim\>\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>>
  the two terms cancel at leading order. The source and the dissipation are
  the same quantity, so estimating them separately gives nothing. To close,
  one would need, for instance, a signed estimate that retains this
  cancellation, or a tail bound for <math|\<Phi\>> obtained independently of
  <math|R>. On one site Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:pl-tails> is such a
  bound: the probability of reaching the clip is at most of order
  <math|exp(-c*q<rsup|2>/\<lambda\>)>, so in expectation the source is
  exponentially small, and the frozen cube is not ruled out there. But that
  bound comes from the posterior moment, and by the remark above it does not
  transfer. The field equation alone cannot replace it, since its restoring
  force is the cube and <math|R> together, which is what we are trying to
  estimate.

  Going further in perturbation theory does not help: any finite depth of
  expansion leaves the same structure (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:pl-depth>),
  and we shall see in a moment that the series of trees stops converging
  inside <math|r<rsub|q>>.

  <paragraph|Integrating the cube> The term responsible for all this is the
  nonlinear transport in (<reference|eq:pl-burgers>). So solve it exactly:
  let <math|f<rsub|q>> solve the Burgers equation without diffusion,

  <\equation>
    \<partial\><rsub|q>f+S<rsup|2>f*f<rprime|'>=0,<space|1em>
    f<rsub|\<infty\>>(x)=-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>.<label|eq:pl-inviscid>
  </equation>

  The characteristics give the solution in closed form.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The integrated cube><label|prop:pl-integrated>For
    <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, let <math|y=y<rsub|q>(x)> be the unique real
    root of <math|q*y+\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>=q*x>. Then the solution of
    (<reference|eq:pl-inviscid>) is

    <\equation>
      f<rsub|q>(x)=-\<lambda\> y<rsub|q>(x)<rsup|3>=q(y<rsub|q>(x)-x),<label|eq:pl-integrated>
    </equation>

    and it has the following properties.

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><strong|Variational form.> <math|y<rsub|q>(x)> is the minimiser
      of <math|<frac|\<lambda\>|4>y<rsup|4>+<frac|q|2>(y-x)<rsup|2>>, and
      <math|f<rsub|q>=-<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|q><rprime|'>> for the minimum
      value <math|<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|q>(x)>.

      <item><strong|Small fields.> <math|f<rsub|q>(x)=q<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>1>(-\<lambda\>/q)<rsup|k>c<rsub|k>x<rsup|2k+1>>
      with <math|c<rsub|k>=<frac|1|2k+1><frac|(3k)!|k!(2k)!>>, so
      <math|f<rsub|q>=-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>+3\<lambda\><rsup|2>S*x<rsup|5>-12\<lambda\><rsup|3>S<rsup|2>x<rsup|7>+\<ldots\>>.
      This is the sum of the tree expansion. Its radius of convergence is
      <math|\|x\|=<frac|2|3>r<rsub|q>>.

      <item><strong|Large fields.> <math|\<lambda\>\|y\|<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>
      q\|x\|>, so <math|f<rsub|q>(x)=-q*x+O(\|x\|<rsup|1/3>)> at fixed
      <math|q>, as for the exact force, and
      <math|f<rsub|q><rprime|'>=-\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>/(1+\<gamma\>
      y<rsup|2>/q)\<leqslant\>0> for all <math|x>.

      <item><strong|Defect.> <math|f<rsub|q>> fails to solve Burgers by the
      diffusion term alone, which is <math|q> times the drift of
      Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:one-site>:

      <\equation>
        E<rsub|q>=<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>f<rsub|q><rprime|''>(x)=q*b<rsub|q>(y<rsub|q>(x)),<space|1em>
        b<rsub|q>(y)=-\<gamma\> y(q+\<gamma\>
        y<rsup|2>)<rsup|-3>.<label|eq:pl-defect>
      </equation>
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Differentiate <math|x=y+\<lambda\> S*y<rsup|3>> with <math|d=1+\<gamma\>
    y<rsup|2>/q>: <math|y<rprime|'>=1/d>,
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|S>y=-\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>/d>,
    <math|f<rprime|'>=-\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>/d> and
    <math|f<rprime|''>=-2\<gamma\> y/d<rsup|3>>. Hence
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|S>f=\<gamma\>\<lambda\>
    y<rsup|5>/d=f*f<rprime|'>>, which is (<reference|eq:pl-inviscid>) as
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>=-S<rsup|2>\<partial\><rsub|S>>. For (a), the
    stationarity condition is <math|\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>+q(y-x)=0>. For (b),
    use Lagrange inversion on <math|y=x-\<lambda\> S*y<rsup|3>>; the nearest
    singularity is where <math|d=0>, that is at <math|y=\<pm\>\<mathi\>
    r<rsub|q>>, <math|x=\<pm\><frac|2|3>\<mathi\> r<rsub|q>>. Finally (c) and
    (d) are read off from the formulas, since <math|d=1/(q*H<rsub|q>(y))>
    with <math|H<rsub|q>(y)=(q+\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>)<rsup|-1>>.
  </proof>

  Item (b) explains why the depth of the expansion is not the issue. The tree
  series has the same complex singularity <math|y=\<pm\>\<mathi\> r<rsub|q>>
  as the one-loop seed of Section<nbsp>12.1, and its truncations at even
  <math|k> point outward at large fields.

  The defect is now bounded for all fields, and it gives a global estimate.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Global error of the integrated cube><label|thm:pl-global>Let
    <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>=3sup<rsub|z>\|z\|(1+3z<rsup|2>)<rsup|-3>>, so
    <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\><frac|1|2>>. For all <math|q\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation>
      sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>\|F<rsub|q>(x)-f<rsub|q>(x)\|\<leqslant\>2C<rsub|\<ast\>>(\<lambda\>/q)<rsup|1/2>.<label|eq:pl-global>
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Use <math|S=1/q> as time, starting from <math|S=0>. Subtracting,
    <math|<wide|R|^>=F-f> solves the linear problem

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-D\><wide|R|^>=<frac|1|2>f<rprime|''>,<space|1em>\<cal-D\>=\<partial\><rsub|S>-<frac|1|2>\<partial\><rsub|x><rsup|2>-F\<partial\><rsub|x>-f<rprime|'>,<space|1em><wide|R|^><rsub|0>=0.
    </equation*>

    The zeroth-order coefficient has the good sign, as
    <math|-f<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>0>. With <math|z=(\<lambda\>
    S)<rsup|1/2>y>, the source obeys <math|<frac|1|2>\|f<rprime|''>\|\<leqslant\>
    C<rsub|\<ast\>>(\<lambda\>/S)<rsup|1/2>>, which is integrable at
    <math|S=0>, so <math|\<beta\>(S)=2C<rsub|\<ast\>>(\<lambda\>
    S)<rsup|1/2>> satisfies <math|\<cal-D\>\<beta\>\<geqslant\><frac|1|2>\|f<rprime|''>\|>.
    To apply the maximum principle on the line, take
    <math|\<chi\>=\<mathe\><rsup|3S>(1+x<rsup|4>)>. Then
    <math|\<cal-D\>\<chi\>\<geqslant\>0>, since
    <math|6x<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>3(1+x<rsup|4>)> and
    <math|x*F(x)\<leqslant\>0>; the last holds because
    <math|F=-\<lambda\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|3>>, and
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|3>> has the sign of <math|x>, the density
    being an even density tilted by <math|\<mathe\><rsup|q*x*y>>. Both
    <math|F> and <math|f> grow at most cubically, uniformly for bounded
    <math|S>: <math|\|f\|\<leqslant\>\<lambda\>\|x\|<rsup|3>> since
    <math|\|y\|\<leqslant\>\|x\|>, and <math|\<lambda\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|6>+q\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|4>-q*x\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|3>=3\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|2>>
    gives <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>(\|x\|+(3S)<rsup|1/2>)<rsup|4>>,
    up to constants, hence <math|\|F\|\<leqslant\>
    C\<lambda\>(\|x\|<rsup|3>+S<rsup|3/2>)>. Also <math|<wide|R|^>> is
    continuous at <math|S=0>, since the posterior concentrates at <math|x>.
    So <math|\<pm\><wide|R|^>-\<beta\>-\<varepsilon\>\<chi\>> is negative on
    the boundary of a large cylinder, hence inside it. Let the cylinder grow
    and then <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0>.
  </proof>

