  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.8par|||>>>|<label|fig:scalar-force>The
  force on one site, for <math|\<lambda\>=1>, <math|q=4>. Left: the frozen
  cube leaves the exact force at <math|x\<sim\>r<rsub|q>>, while the
  integrated cube follows it at all fields. Right: the remainder of the
  frozen cube grows like the cube itself, while the remainder of the
  integrated cube stays below the bound of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:pl-global>.>

  <paragraph|What this says about the choice of equation> The comparison on
  one site is summarised as follows, for <math|\|x\|\<gg\> r<rsub|q>>.

  <padded-center|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-lsep|0.8spc>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-rsep|0.8spc>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|frozen
  cube, clipped corrections>|<cell|integrated cube>>|<row|<cell|growth of
  <math|f>>|<cell|cubic>|<cell|linear, as for <math|F>>>|<row|<cell|remainder
  <math|\|F-f\|>>|<cell|<math|\<sim\>\<lambda\>\|x\|<rsup|3>>>|<cell|<math|\<leqslant\>2C<rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<lambda\>/q|)><rsup|1/2>>>>|<row|<cell|Burgers
  defect>|<cell|<math|3\<lambda\><rsup|2>*S<rsup|2>*x<rsup|5>>>|<cell|<math|q*b<rsub|q>*<around|(|y|)>>,
  bounded by <math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<lambda\><rsup|1/2>*q<rsup|-3/2>>>>>>>>

  <strong|The integrated cube is the forecast coordinate.> The equation
  <math|q*y+\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>=q*x>, at
  <math|x=\<Phi\><rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>/q>, is the coordinate equation of
  Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:one-site>, so
  <math|y<rsub|q>(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)=Y<rsub|q>>. Equations
  (<reference|eq:pl-tweedie>) and (<reference|eq:pl-integrated>) then say
  that <math|F<rsub|q>-f<rsub|q>=q(m<rsub|q>-y<rsub|q>)>: the remainder is
  <math|K<rsub|q>Z<rsub|q>>, and the error <math|Z> of (<reference|eq:YZ>) is
  the posterior mean minus the posterior mode. By (<reference|eq:pl-global>),
  on one site <math|\|Z<rsub|q>\|\<leqslant\>2C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<lambda\><rsup|1/2>q<rsup|-3/2>>
  surely, for all fields.

  <strong|Why Wetterich has no such problem.> In the variable <math|y> the
  bare quartic does not flow at all: all the trees of (b) are contained in
  the map <math|x\<mapsto\> y>. The right side of (<reference|eq:wetterich>)
  is a loop, and the defect <math|e<rsub|q>(y)=-<frac|1|2>[H<rsub|q>(y)-S]>
  of <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>y<rsup|4>> is
  bounded by <math|S/2>. This is why Section<nbsp>12.2 can leave the quartic
  untouched and clip only the corrections: what is clipped consists of loops,
  not of trees whose large-field sum is forced to change the growth of the
  action. This removes the frozen-cube problem specifically: the clipping
  error of Section<nbsp>12.3 is still there, and it is what Lecture<nbsp>6
  pays for.

  <strong|Where the coercivity is.> Compare the same observation written in
  the two coordinates:

  <\equation*>
    q*y+\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>=P<rsub|q>,<space|1em>
    q*x=(q-q<rsub|0>)m<rsub|q>(x)+\<eta\><rsub|q>.
  </equation*>

  In the coordinate <math|y> the cube is genuine, with the known right side
  <math|P<rsub|q>=(q-q<rsub|0>)\<psi\><rsub|q>+\<eta\><rsub|q>>. This is the
  equation <hlink|(13.4)|cqft_lecture6.tm#eq:field> of Lecture<nbsp>6, whose
  coercivity the a priori estimates exploit. In the coordinate <math|x> the
  cube has already been used up, and only a linear restoring force is
  visible.

  <strong|What the model points to.> What the scalar model points to is the
  integration of the cube. The Wetterich equation and the FBSDE of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:YZ> are one way to organise it; another would
  keep the original field, with <math|f<rsup|(0)>> given by the variational
  formula of (a), and work with the defect
  <math|<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>(C<rsub|q><rsup|2>D<rsup|2>f<rsup|(0)>)>. On
  the lattice the variational formula still defines this force, with a
  nonlocal response, but its defect contains the loop contractions, starting
  with the tadpole, and must be renormalised. A complete proof along that
  route is not given in these lectures.

  The scalar model proves less than a no-go theorem. It shows that a global
  estimate treating the remainder of a frozen cube as a small cubic
  perturbation is false. It does not show that every construction based on
  the Polchinski equation fails, nor does it give the asymptotics of the
  force on the lattice. There is also a limit to the integrated cube itself.
  With a quadratic term, the map <math|y\<mapsto\> y+S(\<lambda\>
  y<rsup|3>+r*y)> has derivative <math|1+S(r+3\<lambda\> y<rsup|2>)>, which
  vanishes somewhere when <math|1+S*r\<less\>0>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:pl-negative>). A divergent negative mass
  cannot simply be placed inside this monotone map, and this is one reason
  why the mass correction <math|M<rsub|q>> of (<reference|eq:clipping>) is
  kept apart from the quartic and is required to be small relative to
  <math|K<rsub|q>> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:resolvent>).

  <subsection|The action below the auxiliary scale><label|sec:low-action>

  The action above is built for <math|q\<geqslant\> q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, because
  its expansion needs <math|g<rsub|q>> small. Nothing below
  <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> requires a construction: we freeze it. For
  <math|0\<less\>*q\<less\>*q<rsub|\<ast\>>> set

  <\equation>
    <wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>(y)=<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>(y)+<frac|1|2>(q<rsub|\<ast\>>-q)<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.<label|eq:freeze>
  </equation>

  Then <math|K<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2><wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>+D<rsup|2><wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
  so the coordinate equation <math|K<rsub|q>y+D<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>(y)=P>
  reads <math|K<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>y+D<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>(y)=P>.
  Thus for <math|q\<less\>*q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, with <math|x=C<rsub|q>P>,

  <\equation>
    H<rsub|q>=H<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>,<space|1em>
    y<rsub|q>(x)=y<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>(C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>K<rsub|q>x),<space|1em>
    C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>K<rsub|q>=I-(q<rsub|\<ast\>>-q)C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>.<label|eq:freeze-y>
  </equation>

  The Legendre computation of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:YZ> holds for any
  such family. So the system (<reference|eq:YZ-fbsde>) holds on
  <math|[q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>)>, with <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0> for
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>: <strong|the initial value is deterministic>, and no
  large mass is used. The action is continuous in <math|q> and piecewise
  <math|C<rsup|1>>, which is all that Itô's formula needs. The defect for
  <math|s\<less\>*q<rsub|\<ast\>>> is explicit:

  <\equation>
    e<rsub|s>(y)=-<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>+\<Upsilon\>(y),<space|1em>
    \<Upsilon\>(y)=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>[H<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>(y)-H<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>(0)].<label|eq:e-low>
  </equation>

  Here <math|D\<Upsilon\>> is the trace part of the gradient of
  <math|e<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>; it obeys
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D\<Upsilon\>(y)|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>
  C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>, which is
  part of the proof of the global linear bound of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:source> <cite|FBSDE26>. As
  <math|\<Upsilon\>(0)=0>, uniformly in <math|N>, for
  <math|s\<less\>*q<rsub|\<ast\>>>,

  <\equation>
    \|e<rsub|s>(y)\|\<leqslant\> C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsub|s>(y)|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>
    C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>,<space|1em><math-up|diag>H<rsub|s><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>
    C,<label|eq:low>
  </equation>

  with <math|b=-H*D*e=H(y-D\<Upsilon\>)>. Here we used
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\> p>\<leqslant\>
  C/q<rsub|\<ast\>>>. These are all the properties of
  <math|s\<less\>*q<rsub|\<ast\>>> we use. Note that <math|e> jumps at
  <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>; the jump is harmless, and the measure does not
  depend on <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>. More precisely
  <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>,y,e,b,Y,Z> depend on <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  while, with <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> fixed (<math|b<rsub|N>> then depends on
  <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:counter>),
  <math|\<nu\>>, <math|\<psi\>>, <math|<math-up|Cov>> and the law of <math|P>
  do not.

  <subsection|Summary>

  At the end of this lecture we have, at every lattice cutoff:

  <\itemize>
    <item>a counterterm <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>, a mass path <math|M<rsub|q>>
    and a globally convex action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=U+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,M<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+h<rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>>,
    for <math|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>> and frozen below it, all
    deterministic and constructed without any information on
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>;

    <item>the exact FBSDE started from zero<nbsp><eqref|eq:YZ-fbsde> for the
    approximate forecast <math|Y> and the error <math|Z>, with noise
    coefficients bounded, above <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:resolvent>;

    <item>above <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>, a source
    <math|b=b<rsup|<math-up|b>>+b<rsup|<math-up|l>>>, whose bounded-field
    part is integrable in the backward equation thanks to the two correction
    steps, and whose large-field part is controlled by sixth powers of
    <math|Y> on the large-field set.
  </itemize>

  Lecture<nbsp>6 turns these deterministic bounds into estimates on
  <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>> uniform in <math|N>.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Receiving a centred kernel><label|ex:receive> Let <math|\<Theta\>>
    be a translation-invariant, even kernel with
    <math|\<Theta\>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=0>. With <math|T<rsub|z>> the
    translation by <math|z>, <math|<around|(|T<rsub|z>*f|)><around|(|x|)>=f*<around|(|x+z|)>>,
    and using <math|C<rsub|q>*\<Theta\>=<frac|1|2>*<big|int>\<Theta\><around|(|z|)>*<around|(|T<rsub|z>+T<rsub|-z>-2|)>*C<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z>,
    show that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>*\<Theta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|0.17em>q<rsup|-1/2>*v<rsub|1><around|(|\<Theta\>|)>>
    if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|T<rsub|z>+T<rsub|-z>-2|)>*C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<leqslant\>C*q<rsup|-1/2>*d<around|(|0,z|)>>.
    Why is the absolute row mass of <math|\<Theta\>> not the right quantity?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The scalar clipping error><label|ex:clip-scalar> On one site, let
    <math|h<around|(|y|)>=-\<beta\>*<frac|\<gamma\><rsup|2>|4*q<rsup|2>>*y<rsup|4>>
    (a quartic correction, <math|\<beta\>> a fixed number) and
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>+h<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)>>.
    (a) Show that <math|K<rsub|q>+<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><rprime|''>\<geqslant\>q/2>
    for all <math|y> if <math|\<beta\>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>c*q<rsup|2>>. (b)
    Show that <math|b<rsub|q>=b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>> for
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|q>>. For
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>2*r<rsub|q>> show that
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|q>> is locally constant,
    <math|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=0>, and
    <math|b<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=-\<gamma\>*y*<around|(|q+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>>,
    the drift of the pure quartic. (c) Conclude that on the large-field set
    the error is the whole drift of the trace term: it is comparable, for
    <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<approx\>2*r<rsub|q>>, to the one-site bound
    <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3><around|\||y|\|>> on the whole drift (the
    analogue of <math|<around|(|g<rsub|q>/q|)><around|\||y|\|>>, with
    <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>=q<rsup|-2>> in place of
    <math|q<rsup|-1/2>>), but it vanishes on the ball. Compare
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:source>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Large fields are less noisy>On one site with
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>>, show that
    the quadratic variation of <math|Y> is
    <math|<big|int>H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q> and
    that <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-2>>.
    Simulate the observation process for <math|\<nu\>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>/4>\<mu\><rsup|1/q<rsub|0>>>
    and plot <math|P<rsub|q>>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>/q>,
    <math|Y<rsub|q>> and <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The defect>Check (<reference|eq:pl-defect>) and integrate it: show
    that <math|<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>q<rprime|'>sup\|b<rsub|q<rprime|'>>\|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    q<rprime|'>> equals the right side of (<reference|eq:pl-global>). Why
    does the integration of the cube turn the <math|x<rsup|5>> of
    (<reference|eq:pl-quintic>) into a bounded function?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Depth of the expansion><label|ex:pl-depth>Let <math|U> be any
    approximate potential, <math|U<rsup|c>=U+\<Delta\>>. Show that
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U<rsup|c>)=\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U)+D<rsub|q>\<Delta\>-2B(U,\<Delta\>)-B(\<Delta\>,\<Delta\>)>.
    Which terms are controlled by the smallness of the truncation defect
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U)>? Take <math|U> the tree expansion up to order
    <math|k>, and <math|\<Delta\>> the clipping of its terms beyond the cube.
    What replaces <math|3\<lambda\><rsup|2>S<rsup|2>x<rsup|5>> in
    (<reference|eq:pl-quintic>)?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|A companion identity does not give a sign>For
    <math|U=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>x<rsup|4>> and <math|f=-U<rprime|'>>, let
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|q>=(V<rsub|q>-U)(\<Phi\><rsub|q>)> and <math|R=F-f>.
    Show that <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    \<beta\><rsub|q>=-[\<cal-E\><rsub|q>(U)+<frac|1|2>S<rsup|2>R<rsup|2>]<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    q-S*R<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W<rsub|q>>. Show that in the bracket the
    sextic terms cancel exactly, and that what remains is <math|-\<lambda\>
    S*x<rsup|4>(1+o(1))> as <math|\|x\|\<to\>\<infty\>>. Conclude that the
    bracket does not dominate a positive multiple of
    <math|S<rsup|2>R<rsup|2>>, so this identity is an algebraic cancellation,
    not a coercive estimate.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Negative mass><label|ex:pl-negative>For <math|r\<less\>0>, let
    <math|y\<mapsto\> y+S(\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>+r*y)>. Show that it is
    monotone if and only if <math|1+S*r\<geqslant\>0>. What happens to the
    minimiser in the variational formula when <math|1+S*r\<less\>0>, and to
    the solution of (<reference|eq:pl-inviscid>) with
    <math|f<rsub|\<infty\>>=-\<lambda\> x<rsup|3>-r*x>? Why does this not
    contradict the existence of the exact, smooth force <math|F<rsub|q>>?
  </exercise>

